[PERSISTENT EXPERIENCE GUIDANCE]
## Consolidated Experience from Similar Tasks
Consolidated lessons from the process experiences: (1) Manipulation actions have strict preconditions. Before attempting to pick up an object, verify that the target object exists, is near the robot, and is within reach; if not, navigate to the object first. (2) Before attempting to place an object, verify that the robot is already holding that object; if not, complete a pick-up action first. (3) Before attempting to move, verify that the intended path is clear of obstacles, especially after rotating; if feedback indicates a blockage, replan the route. (4) Do not assume an action will succeed based only on its name—check all preconditions and use environment feedback to detect failures. (5) When an action fails or a precondition is unmet, address the missing subgoal (navigate, pick, clear path) before retrying the original action.

## Preliminary Thinking
For tasks involving object delivery or rearrangement: 1) Identify the target object and the destination. 2) Locate the target object and navigate to it if it is not already within reach. 3) Verify the object is near and reachable, then execute the pick-up action and confirm the robot is holding it. 4) Plan a path to the destination, checking that the route is clear of obstacles; if blocked, replan or navigate around the obstacle. 5) Navigate to the destination while maintaining the grasp. 6) Verify the robot is still holding the object, then execute the place action at the destination. 7) Confirm the object has been placed successfully, or if any step fails, inspect the cause, satisfy the missing precondition, and retry.
[/PERSISTENT EXPERIENCE GUIDANCE]